; ==== a byte from the TAPE PREFIX enters before any from the wire ====
; cSHAKE and KMAC put whole bytepad blocks in front of the message  and
; sponge reads its message from the wire  so the prefix has to come from
; somewhere else; It comes from a buffer above the frame  consumed by a
; SECOND CONVEYOR feeding the first: the head of the buffer enters the
; block and the buffer slides down one  exactly as the block itself does;
; So no block machinery is new here  only where the next byte comes from;
; is any of the prefix still to come
R78
  [-]
L5
  [-R3+R1+L4]
R4
  [-L4+R4]
L1
  [[-]R2[-]+L2]
; and the high byte through the same cell
L2
  [-R2+R1+L3]
R3
  [-L3+R3]
L1
  [[-]R2[-]+L2]
; the else arm is armed  because the prefix arm clears it
L1
  [-]
  +
R3
[
  [-]
; the else does not fire  and the head of the prefix enters the block
L3
  [-]
R6
  [-L81+R81]
